 \newcommand{\pars}[1]{\left(\,{#1}\,\right)}
 \newcommand{\partiald}[3][]{\frac{\partial^{#1} #2}{\partial #3^{#1}}}
 \newcommand{\root}[2][]{\,\sqrt[#1]{\,{#2}\,}\,}
 \newcommand{\totald}[3][]{\frac{\mathrm{d}^{#1} #2}{\mathrm{d} #3^{#1}}}
 \newcommand{\verts}[1]{\left\vert\,{#1}\,\right\vert}$
\begin{align}
\oint_{\verts{z} = 2}\root{z^{4} - z}\,\dd z & =
\,\,\,\stackrel{z\ \mapsto\ 1/z}{=}\,\,\,
\oint_{\verts{z} = 1/2}{\root{1 - z^{3}} \over z^{4}}\,\dd z
\end{align}
There is a pole of order  four  at $\ds{z = 0}$. The residue at such pole is equal to $\ds{\color{#f00}{-\,{1 \over 2}}}$ which is straightforward evaluated by expanding the square root in powers of $\ds{z}$. Namely, 

$\ds{\root{1 - z^{3}} = 1 + \pars{\color{#f00}{-\,{1 \over 2}}}z^{3} + \,\mrm{O}\pars{z^{6}}}$

$$
\oint_{\verts{z} = 2}\root{z^{4} - z}\,\dd z = 2\pi\ic\pars{-\,{1 \over 2}} = \bbx{-\pi\ic}
$$ 
